+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edit Policy
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers share 12 tablets across 24 people. Tablets can be offline for up to 48 hours. Two workers can independently edit the same visit — comparing field notes and correcting each other's observations is normal practice. Their edits arrive at the server without prior coordination.
+
+The record store must define what a "current visit" means when independently edited copies arrive, and what the server does with the copy it does not promote. This decision sets the submission contract that tablet-sync and review-export must implement, so it is resolved before those nodes are designed.
+
+An inherited constraint from the HLD applies to all options: original submissions must never be discarded.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **No silent data loss.** An option that could discard or overwrite a submitted edit without the submitting worker or a coordinator being aware of it is eliminated. Derived from the HLD tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Scientific integrity of the output record** (major) — When a policy combines field edits from multiple submissions, does it risk producing a record no single observer submitted and no coordinator reviewed? The inspectability tenet (*Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*) is at stake here.
+- **Coordinator review workload** (major) — How many queue items does each concurrent edit generate? The team has two coordinators and a Friday export deadline. Excess review work either delays the export or degrades review quality.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** (moderate) — How much logic must be written and kept current as the visit schema evolves? The HLD tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* applies here: complex server-side rules are a maintenance surface that would require ongoing technical attention.
+- **Tablet and server submission contract complexity** (moderate) — How much must the tablet retain about in-flight visits? How complex is the server's ingestion path?
+- **Submission payload size** (minor) — At roughly 6,000 visits per season across 12 tablets with intermittent connectivity, payload size has some operational weight, though the visit schema is small enough that this is not a deciding factor.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Option A: Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission carries a `base_revision_id` — the ID of the visit revision the worker started from. The server checks whether that base matches the current head. If it does, the submission advances the head. If it does not, the submission is stored as a competing revision and the visit becomes conflicted. A coordinator reviews competing revisions side-by-side and selects one as the new head. No field-level automatic combination takes place.
+
+Tablet-sync needs to retain the head revision ID from the last fetch and include it with every submission. The server's ingestion logic is a single comparison: base matches head, or does not.
+
+Review-export sees a set of conflicted visits. Each conflicted visit has a fixed number of whole-visit snapshots to compare. All conflicted visits are held out of the export until resolved.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- No silent data loss: **passes** — every submission is stored; the coordinator selects, not discards.
+- Scientific integrity: **strong** — no automatic field combination occurs. Every exported record is either a single observer's submission or a coordinator-chosen submission. The path from submitted observation to exported value is always one hop.
+- Coordinator review workload: **weak** — two workers correcting unrelated fields (one fixes an observer name, another adds a missing species) create a conflict requiring coordinator review. The review queue is larger than necessary.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong** — no merge rules exist to write or maintain. The ingestion path is a revision-ID comparison. Schema changes do not require rule updates.
+- Submission contract complexity: **strong** — tablet retains one UUID per in-flight visit (the head revision ID). Submission is the full visit payload. Server ingestion is a single lookup.
+- Submission payload size: **weak** — full visit payload on every submission, even when one field changed.
+
+*The essential trade: a bigger review queue in exchange for zero automatic combination logic and complete traceability.*
+
+### Option B: Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission carries its base snapshot and a structured change record: which fields changed, from what value, to what value. The server performs a three-way merge. Disjoint field changes are combined automatically into a new head revision with no coordinator involvement. Overlapping field changes — both workers edited the same field — go to coordinator review.
+
+Merge rules must be defined per field type: scalar fields (observation date, notes), list fields (observer names), and structured list entries (species cover, where each entry has a species code and cover class). A coordinator-authored resolution path is also required for the overlap case.
+
+Tablet-sync retains a base snapshot for each in-flight visit and computes the change record on submission. Review-export sees a smaller conflict queue (only overlapping field edits) but each conflicted record has field-level provenance to display.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- No silent data loss: **passes** — original submissions are retained; merged records are derived but the inputs are kept.
+- Scientific integrity: **partial** — disjoint field edits combine correctly when fields are scientifically independent. However, some field pairs are scientifically related in ways the merge rules cannot detect from field names alone. A species identification correction and its cover estimate correction are related: if two workers each correct one of the pair from different starting points, a disjoint merge produces a record that mixes values from incompatible states. The merge produces a plausible-looking record with no coordinator review and no visible flag. This error enters the reviewed CSV.
+- Coordinator review workload: **strong** — only overlapping edits reach the review queue. Most concurrent edits are to unrelated fields and resolve automatically. Queue size is significantly smaller.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak** — merge rules must cover every field type and combination. Species entries are the most complex (code+cover are a coupled pair). Rules must be updated when the visit schema changes. A gap or bug in the rules silently produces incorrect combined records with no coordinator touchpoint.
+- Submission contract complexity: **weak** — tablet must retain the full base snapshot per in-flight visit and compute a structured diff before submission. Server ingestion runs the three-way merge algorithm per field type.
+- Submission payload size: **strong** — change-record submissions are smaller than full-visit submissions.
+
+*The essential trade: a smaller review queue in exchange for merge rules that must be authored, maintained, and trusted to cover all scientifically meaningful field relationships.*
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions (Option A).**
+
+The major criteria point in opposite directions — scientific integrity favors A, coordinator workload favors B — so the decision turns on the relative cost of each option's failure mode.
+
+Option B's failure mode is a merged record that no single observer submitted and no coordinator reviewed, containing values from incompatible scientific states. This failure is silent: it produces a plausible-looking record that clears the review queue and enters the Friday export. The science partner receives it without a flag. Detection requires post-export comparison against original submission data; correction requires a reissued export.
+
+Option A's failure mode is a review queue item that should not have been a conflict — two unrelated field corrections from different workers. This failure is noisy: it appears in the coordinator's queue, can be resolved before export, and is visible to everyone. The cost is coordinator time. The cost scales with the frequency of unrelated concurrent edits, which is bounded and predictable.
+
+Noisy failure modes are preferable to silent ones in a scientific data context. The inspectability tenet (*Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*) applies not just to the mechanism but to the error case: a corrupt record that entered the export is harder to explain to a science partner than a delayed export caused by a review queue backlog.
+
+Option B's workload advantage is also contingent on the merge rules covering all field relationships correctly. Species entries are the highest-risk case: the relationship between species code and cover class is domain knowledge the server does not hold. Extending the rules to cover it — and testing that coverage — is ongoing technical work with no obvious termination, and it requires someone who understands both the data model and the biology. That person is not a systems administrator, but the work is maintenance of the same kind.
+
+**What this commits to downstream:**
+
+- Tablet-sync must retain the head revision ID per visit and attach it to every submission. On a clean submission, the acceptance response supplies the new head ID. On a competing submission, the acceptance response signals `conflicted`; the tablet has no built-in mechanism to resolve it.
+- Review-export must support a conflict queue showing all competing revisions for a visit, a side-by-side comparison view, and a coordinator selection operation. Conflicted visits are excluded from the export-eligible set until resolved.
+- The review queue will include some conflicts that a field-aware merge would have resolved automatically. This is the known and accepted cost of the decision.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and is taken *against* reducing coordinator workload where the two conflict.
